  "image_name": "2ce6d20cd19c33c1bb72f9f5ad443c93.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic shows how inflammation in an atherosclerotic artery can destabilize a plaque and promote formation of an intraluminal thrombus. The vessel wall is drawn in layers, from the blood-filled lumen and endothelium through the intima and media to the adventitia. A large atherosclerotic plaque bulges into the lumen, narrowing the vessel.\n\nThe most prominent feature is a dense clot-like structure on the plaque surface, associated with neutrophils near the endothelium. Within and around the plaque, macrophages and other immune cells accumulate, indicating strong immune cell infiltration. Additional immune cells, including monocytes, T cells, and B cells, appear to enter from the adventitial side, including through the vasa vasorum.\n\nArrows and labels emphasize several damaging pathways: cytokines, oxygen-derived free radicals, and proteases act on the underlying vessel wall. These signals are linked to vascular smooth muscle cell phenotype changes and apoptosis, as well as matrix degradation in the media. Together, the figure conveys that plaque inflammation involves both luminal and outer-wall immune recruitment, leading to structural weakening of the arterial wall and thrombosis on top of the plaque.",
